---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="The signup is slow. What moves first?" description="Practice card. A slow controller action with a clear culprit, four candidate fixes. Drills 'use background jobs for things the user doesn't need to see complete'.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Scaling · Card 7</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">The signup is slow. What moves first?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A signup page that takes 3 seconds to respond. The user is waiting on something they don't need to see complete.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The action
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">The user posts the signup form. The page takes about 3 seconds before redirecting.</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`def create
  @user = User.new(user_params)
  if @user.save
    WelcomeMailer.with(user: @user).welcome_email.deliver_now
    SegmentClient.track_signup(@user)
    Slack.notify("#new-signups", "\#{@user.email} just signed up")
    redirect_to dashboard_path
  else
    render :new
  end
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Which is the right first move to make this signup feel fast to the user?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Add a loading spinner with JavaScript so the wait feels intentional.',
              explanation: 'A spinner makes a 3s wait visible, not absent. The user is still blocked. Solve the underlying wait first; UX polish second.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'Move the email, the Segment event, and the Slack notification into background jobs. The action redirects as soon as the user is saved.',
              explanation: 'None of those three side effects need to complete before the user sees the dashboard. They are slow, networked, and failure-tolerant — perfect candidates. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">deliver_later</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">SegmentJob.perform_later</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">SlackJob.perform_later</code>. The controller returns in 50ms instead of 3s.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'Wrap the three side effects in a Thread so they run in parallel.',
              explanation: 'Threads in a request can cause connection-pool exhaustion, lose exceptions, and don\'t survive the request finishing cleanly. Background jobs exist for exactly this reason: they\'re Threads-done-right with retries, observability, and isolation.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Increase the Puma request timeout so 3s stops feeling like an outlier.',
              explanation: 'Hiding slow responses by raising the timeout doesn\'t make them fast. It also worsens degradation: under load, Puma workers stay busy longer, queue grows, everyone is slower.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>The question to ask of any side effect inside a controller action: <strong>does the user need to see this complete before getting their response?</strong></p><ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4 space-y-1"><li>If yes (saving their data, returning their answer, reading what they need to see) — synchronous.</li><li>If no (sending an email, hitting a third-party analytics service, posting to Slack, generating a thumbnail) — background job.</li></ul><p>Solid Queue, Sidekiq, Good Job, and Delayed Job all support <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">perform_later</code> as the ActiveJob entry point. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">deliver_later</code> on a mailer is the same pattern. The action returns immediately; the side effects run on a worker process, with retries, with proper error handling, without keeping the user waiting.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/senior-scaling-background-jobs"
          theoryLabel="Scaling · Background Jobs as a Scaling Tool"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-scaling-concurrency-race" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 6</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-scaling-bulk-operations" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 8 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
